{"preference":"A","margin":5,"reasons":"The purpose is to revise for actionability without changing what the summary says. A keeps the design-supported claim: a non-randomized internal pilot comparison, an explicit selection-bias caveat, the evaluator's quote reproduced faithfully, and a recommendation to call results preliminary. A also gives the board concrete numbers (4,218 students, 37 schools, $2.9M, about $688 per student). B turns a non-randomized comparison into proof of causation, drops the selection caveat, alters the evaluator's quote from 'do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains' to 'show that tutoring works', rounds 4,218 up to 'nearly 5,000', adds an unsupported '38 percent advantage' framing, and claims a future randomized study will 'confirm' what is already 'clear'. That is an unsupported causal inference and a hedge that disappeared in revision, which could mislead a budget vote. The 3-point difference is also never accompanied by a standard error, effect size, or baseline equivalence in either candidate.","objections":[{"candidate":"B","category":"inference","quote":"a 38 percent advantage that proves the model works","claim":"A non-randomized difference in average gains (11 vs 8 points) cannot prove the model works; the comparison is confounded by selection into tutoring, and no uncertainty or baseline equivalence is reported. The 38 percent figure is a relative gain ratio of 3 points on 8, not an effect size.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"B","category":"uncertainty","quote":"the results are encouraging and show that tutoring works.","claim":"The evaluator's quoted conclusion is reversed from the source ('do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains'), misattributing a causal claim to the evaluator.","severity":"critical"},{"candidate":"B","category":"uncertainty","quote":"will confirm what the first year has already made clear.","claim":"Presupposes the outcome of the planned randomized study and treats a pilot as settled; the purpose of the RCT is to test the causal claim, not confirm it.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"B","category":"argument","quote":"nearly 5,000 students","claim":"Distorts the reported sample size of 4,218; rounding up by roughly 18 percent overstates reach.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"B","category":"inference","quote":"The effect was unmistakable.","claim":"Describes an effect where only an uncontrolled difference in gains exists; the selection caveat is gone.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"B","category":"argument","quote":"delivered results that larger programs have struggled to match","claim":"No comparison to other programs is presented; unsupported benchmark claim.","severity":"major"},{"candidate":"A","category":"uncertainty","quote":"tutored students gained 11 scale-score points on average; students who were not tutored gained 8 points.","claim":"No variance, effect size, or evidence of baseline equivalence is given, so the reader cannot judge whether the 3-point gap is meaningful even descriptively. Minor, since the caveat is stated.","severity":"minor"},{"candidate":"A","category":"audience_fit","quote":"approximately $688 per student","claim":"Cost is given but not paired with the size of any gain, so the board gets no cost-per-point sense. Minor omission.","severity":"minor"}],"allegations":[{"candidate":"B","quote":"nearly 5,000 students in grades 1 through 3","claim":"Source figure appears to be 4,218 students (per Candidate A), so 'nearly 5,000' misstates it."},{"candidate":"B","quote":"the results are encouraging and show that tutoring works.","claim":"Alters the evaluator quote relative to the original, which per Candidate A reads 'encouraging but do not yet show that tutoring caused the gains'."}],"out_of_lane_notes":"B is also narrative-heavy and omits a plain 'what happens next' guidance for the vote. A's structure with bold labels suits a one-read board audience."}